('127.100.d.7.C',3,'After assembling an exterior wall on the deck, it is:','["Moved to the roof", "Burned", "Raised into position and braced", "Left flat"]'::jsonb,2,'Walls are tilted up.','original'),
('127.100.d.7.C',4,'Plumbing an erected wall means checking that it is:','["Dry", "Long", "Horizontal", "Vertical"]'::jsonb,3,'Plumb equals vertical.','original'),
('127.100.d.7.D',3,'Plywood or OSB sheathing on exterior walls helps to resist:','["Racking forces", "Sound", "Sunlight", "Plants"]'::jsonb,0,'Sheathing stiffens walls.','original'),
('127.100.d.7.D',4,'Wall sheathing nails are typically spaced:','["24 inches everywhere", "6 inches on edges and 12 inches in the field", "1 inch", "Only at corners"]'::jsonb,1,'Common nailing schedule.','original'),
('127.100.d.7.E',3,'When laying out an exterior wall, the carpenter marks the plate for:','["Grass", "Roof pitch", "Stud positions and openings", "Paint colors"]'::jsonb,2,'Layout begins on plates.','original'),
('127.100.d.7.E',4,'Temporary bracing on a freshly erected wall is kept until:','["The roof is painted", "The next day only", "Lunch", "Sheathing and permanent framing are installed"]'::jsonb,3,'Braces hold plumb until the wall is stabilized.','original'),
('127.100.d.7.F',3,'Wood framing next to masonry walls often uses:','["Furring strips", "Rebar", "Glass", "Shingles"]'::jsonb,0,'Furring attaches to masonry.','original'),
('127.100.d.7.F',4,'A wood plate set on a concrete block wall should be:','["Plain pine", "Pressure-treated and anchored", "Loose", "Painted only"]'::jsonb,1,'Treatment prevents decay.','original'),
('127.100.d.7.G',3,'Metal studs are lighter and are:','["Inconsistent", "Rot prone", "Noncombustible", "Combustible"]'::jsonb,2,'Steel does not burn.','original'),
('127.100.d.7.G',4,'Metal studs have punched holes (knockouts) so that:','["They can be seen", "They can rot", "They can float", "Wires and pipes can pass through"]'::jsonb,3,'Knockouts permit utilities.','original'),
('127.100.d.7.H',3,'Ceiling joists are cut to length and installed to tie the walls and support the:','["Ceiling finish", "Windows", "Door", "Footing"]'::jsonb,0,'Joists carry ceiling loads.','original'),
('127.100.d.7.H',4,'Ceiling joists are usually installed:','["Perpendicular to rafters", "Parallel to the rafters", "On the roof", "Under the floor"]'::jsonb,1,'They often run with rafters.','original'),
('127.100.d.7.I',3,'To estimate wall studs on a 32-foot wall at 16 inches on center, the number of spaces is:','["12", "32", "24", "16"]'::jsonb,2,'384 / 16 = 24.','original'),
('127.100.d.7.I',4,'Add studs for corners and openings after counting:','["The shingles", "Nothing", "The paint", "The regular spacing"]'::jsonb,3,'Extras are added.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.A',3,'The ''span'' of a roof is the:','["Distance between outside walls", "Height of the ridge", "Width of an overhang", "Length of a rafter"]'::jsonb,0,'Span is the total width.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.A',4,'The vertical rise divided by the run gives the roof''s:','["Weight", "Pitch (slope)", "Color", "Cost"]'::jsonb,1,'Pitch is rise over run.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.B',3,'The board at the peak of a gable roof is the:','["Sill", "Soffit", "Ridge board", "Fascia"]'::jsonb,2,'The ridge connects rafters.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.B',4,'On a hip roof, the diagonal members at corners are:','["Studs", "Joists", "Headers", "Hip rafters"]'::jsonb,3,'Hip rafters run to corners.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.C',3,'Rafter length can be figured from the rise and run using the:','["Pythagorean theorem", "Mean", "Quadratic formula", "Median"]'::jsonb,0,'Right triangle relationship.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.C',4,'For a roof with a 6 in 12 pitch and 12 feet of run, the rise is:','["12 feet", "6 feet", "3 feet", "18 feet"]'::jsonb,1,'6/12 x 12 = 6.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.D',3,'A common truss shape with webs in a W pattern is called a:','["Flat slab", "Box girder", "Fink truss", "Column"]'::jsonb,2,'Fink trusses have a W web pattern.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.D',4,'Roof trusses are designed by engineers to carry:','["Only snow", "Only wind", "No load", "Roof loads and span the building"]'::jsonb,3,'Trusses are engineered.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.E',3,'The speed square''s pivot point is used to mark:','["Rafter angles", "Stair rails", "Light levels", "Door swings"]'::jsonb,0,'Pitch scales set angles.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.E',4,'A framing square can be used with a calculator to:','["Cut tile", "Compute rafter lengths and cuts", "Paint trim", "Mix mud"]'::jsonb,1,'Squares and calculators work together.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.F',3,'Roof sheathing materials include plywood and:','["Paper", "Carpet", "OSB", "Tile"]'::jsonb,2,'Both panel types are common.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.F',4,'Spaced board sheathing is often used under:','["Concrete", "Asphalt shingles only", "Membrane roofs", "Wood shakes"]'::jsonb,3,'Gaps allow air flow.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.G',3,'A gable end vent opening is framed to allow:','["Attic ventilation", "Tool storage", "Daylight only", "Sound"]'::jsonb,0,'Ventilation reduces moisture.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.G',4,'The framing around a gable vent includes:','["Joists only", "Headers and cripples", "Rafters only", "Nothing"]'::jsonb,1,'The opening is framed like a window.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.H',3,'Before lifting trusses, the crew should plan:','["Nothing", "A picnic", "Bracing and a lifting method", "A paint scheme"]'::jsonb,2,'Safe erection needs planning.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.H',4,'Trusses must be braced to prevent:','["Sound", "Painting", "Insulation", "Toppling during installation"]'::jsonb,3,'Temporary braces hold alignment.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.I',3,'A roof opening such as a skylight is framed with:','["Double headers and trimmers", "Tape", "Single boards", "No framing"]'::jsonb,0,'Opening needs reinforcement.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.I',4,'The size of a roof opening is based on the product''s:','["Price", "Rough opening dimensions", "Color", "Brand slogan"]'::jsonb,1,'Follow manufacturer specs.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.J',3,'To estimate roof sheathing, a carpenter finds the roof''s:','["Door color", "Window count", "Surface area", "Wall height"]'::jsonb,2,'Area is divided by panel area.','original'),
('127.100.d.8.J',4,'If a roof surface is 1,200 square feet and panels cover 32 square feet each, how many panels are needed (rounded up)?','["40", "37", "32", "38"]'::jsonb,3,'1,200 / 32 = 37.5; round up to 38.','original'),
('127.100.d.9.A',3,'Portland cement is a fine powder that becomes:','["A hard binder when mixed with water", "A liquid", "A gas", "A fabric"]'::jsonb,0,'It hydrates and hardens.','original'),
('127.100.d.9.A',4,'Cement is stored in a:','["Wet yard", "Dry location", "Pool", "Shower"]'::jsonb,1,'Moisture ruins cement.','original'),
('127.100.d.9.B',3,'Concrete is a mixture of cement, water, sand, and:','["Paint", "Steel wool", "Gravel", "Cloth"]'::jsonb,2,'Aggregates add bulk.','original'),
('127.100.d.9.B',4,'A lower water-cement ratio generally results in:','["Faster rot", "No change", "Weaker concrete", "Stronger concrete"]'::jsonb,3,'Less water means higher strength.','original'),
('127.100.d.9.C',3,'A 10-foot by 10-foot slab 4 inches thick requires about how many cubic feet?','["33.3", "100", "40", "400"]'::jsonb,0,'10 x 10 x 1/3 = 33.3.','original'),
('127.100.d.9.C',4,'Convert 54 cubic feet to cubic yards.','["5", "2", "3", "1"]'::jsonb,1,'54 / 27 = 2.','original'),
('127.100.d.9.D',3,'Rebar is placed in concrete because concrete is weak in:','["Compression", "Color", "Tension", "Weight"]'::jsonb,2,'Steel resists pulling forces.','original'),
('127.100.d.9.D',4,'Fiber additives in concrete help to:','["Increase water", "Add color", "Add weight", "Reduce shrinkage cracks"]'::jsonb,3,'Fibers control cracking.','original'),
('127.100.d.9.E',3,'A spread footing is wider than the wall above to:','["Distribute the load over more soil", "Hold paint", "Reduce depth", "Save concrete"]'::jsonb,0,'Wide bases reduce soil pressure.','original'),
('127.100.d.9.E',4,'A footing must be placed below the:','["Fence line", "Frost line", "Roof line", "Water line"]'::jsonb,1,'Frost heave can damage footings.','original'),
('127.100.d.9.F',3,'The parts of a wall form include sheathing, studs, and:','["Paint", "Shingles", "Wales", "Hinges"]'::jsonb,2,'Wales stiffen the form.','original'),
('127.100.d.9.F',4,'A form tie holds opposite form faces:','["Apart", "At random", "At the roof", "Together at the proper spacing"]'::jsonb,3,'Ties set wall thickness.','original'),
('127.100.d.9.G',3,'On a concrete job, eye protection is needed because of:','["Splashing wet concrete and flying debris", "Dim light", "Quiet noise", "Cold air only"]'::jsonb,0,'Wet cement irritates eyes.','original'),
('127.100.d.9.G',4,'Form collapse is prevented by:','["Skipping ties", "Proper bracing and following the form design", "Speeding", "Hoping"]'::jsonb,1,'Bracing resists concrete pressure.','original'),
('127.100.d.9.H',3,'Reinforcement is held in place within a form with:','["Tape", "Rope", "Chairs and tie wire", "Glue"]'::jsonb,2,'Chairs set cover.','original'),
('127.100.d.9.H',4,'Checking that a form is plumb before the pour prevents:','["Cracks of color", "Fast curing", "Painting", "A crooked finished wall"]'::jsonb,3,'Plumb forms give straight walls.','original'),
('127.100.d.10.A',3,'A window that does not open is a:','["Fixed window", "Hopper", "Slider", "Casement"]'::jsonb,0,'Fixed windows are stationary.','original'),
('127.100.d.10.A',4,'A double-hung window opens by sliding:','["Left and right", "Sashes up and down", "Inward", "Outward"]'::jsonb,1,'Double-hung sashes are vertical sliders.','original'),
('127.100.d.10.B',3,'Parts of a window installation include the head, jamb, and:','["Joist", "Rafter", "Sill", "Sole plate"]'::jsonb,2,'Frame parts surround the sash.','original'),
('127.100.d.10.B',4,'Flashing is installed to keep water out at the:','["Interior only", "Ceiling", "Floor", "Top and sides of the window"]'::jsonb,3,'Flashing sheds water.','original'),
('127.100.d.10.C',3,'A window must be installed:','["Plumb, level, and square", "Loose", "Crooked", "Unsealed"]'::jsonb,0,'Correct fit ensures operation.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
join public.teks_documents d on d.id = i.document_id and d.subject = 'Career Development' and d.language = 'en'
